        <h1>About</h1>
        <p>I come from a family of makers, artists, and business owners. As a child I dreamt first of becoming a magician, then of designing chairs. And while neither of those dreams came true, I didn’t land far off.</p>
        <p>I now live in Folkestone, on the Kent coast, but I was born in Italy in a land called Ciociaria. These days, I spend my time <a href="/studio/">making complex stories a little less complex</a>, and drawing people closer to good products, good services and good talent. </p>
        <div class="profile-picture_inner grid-cols-2 has-my-xs">
            <img src="./assets/images/francesco-studio-shot.png" alt="Francesco Imola" width="2560" height="2560" class="ratio-1-1">
            <div></div>
        </div>
        <p>I graduated in Digital Arts from the University of Greenwich, which is where much of this started, and then spent six years between cultural institutions and the Film & TV industry. I also <a href="/writing/">write</a>, and volunteer with <a href="https://openartfolke.com" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open Art Folke</a> to support fellow artists. And I <a href="/art/">make art</a> of my own, with words and pixels.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="flow has-gap-s">
        <h2>Philosophy</h2>
        <p>We live in turbulent times. There is an overabundance of information, and in the midst of it I see designers taking on facilitator roles, aka helping people communicate.</p>
        <p>When it comes to marketing, I specialise in the trust-based kind—or the art of building credibility and long-term relationships by being honest, helpful and consistent with your audience.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="flow has-gap-s">
        <h2>I'm an agency, I want to hire you</h2>
            <p>It takes all sorts to make a world, and thank goodness for that! I keep an open door policy: I'm open to white-label or overflow briefs from agencies, and I'll consider in-house roles.</p>
            <a href="/collaborate/" data-icon="arrow-right">Here's what I can offer you</a>
